Signed-off-by: Piotr Oniszczuk <piotr.oniszczuk@gmail.com>



From cce5a4d0b1ea623ff3c06df8dd3949200d567a27 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Piotr Oniszczuk <piotr.oniszczuk@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 09:12:16 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] PM / devfreq: Keep the resume frequency across suspend

A device with an opp-suspend frequency, like the RK3528 GPU handled by
lima, which calls devfreq_suspend_device() and devfreq_resume_device()
from its runtime PM callbacks, gets stuck at its suspend frequency when
using the performance (or userspace) governor:

- devfreq_suspend_device() switches to the suspend frequency through
  devfreq_set_target(), which also stores it as the resume frequency,
  overwriting the frequency used before the suspend,
- devfreq_resume_device() then "restores" the suspend frequency, and
  the performance and userspace governors don't reevaluate the target
  frequency on DEVFREQ_GOV_RESUME, unlike the polling governors.

So after the first runtime suspend of the GPU, it stays at 300 MHz
forever.

Don't update the resume frequency while the device is suspended, so
the switch to the suspend frequency no longer overwrites it.

Also, while the device is suspended, don't program a new target
frequency, e.g. when switching governors or changing min_freq, as the
hardware may not be accessible (on RK3528 setting a GPU rate above
300 MHz with the GPU power domain off can hang the system). Only record
it as the frequency to restore on resume instead.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 drivers/devfreq/devfreq.c | 16 +++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 15 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/devfreq/devfreq.c b/drivers/devfreq/devfreq.c
index 82dd9a43d..e057fcdb1 100644
--- a/drivers/devfreq/devfreq.c
+++ b/drivers/devfreq/devfreq.c
@@ -381,7 +381,11 @@ static int devfreq_set_target(struct devfreq *devfreq, unsigned long new_freq,
 
 	devfreq->previous_freq = new_freq;
 
-	if (devfreq->suspend_freq)
+	/*
+	 * Don't let the switch to the suspend frequency overwrite the
+	 * frequency to restore on resume.
+	 */
+	if (devfreq->suspend_freq && !atomic_read(&devfreq->suspend_count))
 		devfreq->resume_freq = new_freq;
 
 	return err;
@@ -423,6 +427,16 @@ int devfreq_update_target(struct devfreq *devfreq, unsigned long freq)
 		flags |= DEVFREQ_FLAG_LEAST_UPPER_BOUND; /* Use LUB */
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * While the device is suspended it stays at its suspend frequency,
+	 * and its clocks may not even be accessible, so only update the
+	 * frequency to restore on resume.
+	 */
+	if (devfreq->suspend_freq && atomic_read(&devfreq->suspend_count)) {
+		devfreq->resume_freq = freq;
+		return 0;
+	}
+
 	return devfreq_set_target(devfreq, freq, flags);
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL(devfreq_update_target);
